{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 40, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of St Gilles and ruled as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Toron. \n\nHe married Eschiva of Armenia and had a son named Bohemond IV King of Antioch. *Family*\n\nIn 1153 Raymond III Count of Tripoli married Eschiva of Armenia and obtained the County of Tripoli as her dowry. The County of Tripoli was a Crusader state established by Raymond II Count of Tripoli in 1104. *Establishment of the County*\n\nThe County of Tripoli was a strategic location for the Crusaders as it was the gateway to the Middle East and protected the Kingdom of Jerusalem from Muslim attacks. Raymond III Count of Tripoli strengthened the County of Tripoli's defenses and established a strong relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem. \n\nIn 1164 Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a key player in the Battle of Hattin. He played a crucial role in the Crusader victory at the Battle of Montgisard in 1177. The victory at Montgisard was a significant turning point in the Crusader campaign against the Muslim forces in the Holy Land. \n\nIn 1184 Raymond III Count of Tripoli captured the city of Beirut from the Muslim forces. However the city was later lost in 1187 to Saladin the Muslim leader who united Egypt and Syria under his rule. \n\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was killed in 1187 during the Battle of the Horns of Hama. His death marked the beginning of the end of the County of Tripoli as a Crusader state. The County of Tripoli was eventually absorbed into the Kingdom of Armenia.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.014390614}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00016938205}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0010648064}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.1300828}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.349725e-05}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.00021